# Build context is exactly what a runner needs: the `.claude` overlay, the admin
# policy, and the writing contract's rules. Exclude everything, then re-include only
# those, so the context stays small and a new top-level directory in the repo cannot
# silently enlarge the image.
*
!.claude/agents
!.claude/skills
!scripts/managed-settings.json

# The writing contract's gate. `writing/styles` is 144K of rule files, and only the
# rules, one configuration template and the wrapper are re-included: the evals,
# fixtures and documentation beside them are a person's inputs, not a runner's.
!writing/styles
!writing/templates/consumer.vale.ini
!scripts/sei-writing-lint

# Vale fetches the third-party packages into the styles tree and .gitignore excludes
# them, so a checkout does not carry them. `vale sync` in Dockerfile.runner puts them
# in the image. Keeping a locally synced copy out of the context makes the built image
# identical whether or not the builder had run Vale.
writing/styles/write-good

# Per-skill scratch state is gitignored; keep it out of the image layer too.
.claude/skills/*/state

# settings.json is deliberately NOT shipped.
#
# It carries a `permissions.allow` pre-approval list, guarded in CI by
# scripts/verify-agent-permissions.sh. Seeding it into $HOME would place it in the
# emptyDir, which the agent can write, so the agent could rewrite it to widen its own
# allowances — an allow-list its subject can edit provides no assurance. Omitting it
# pre-approves nothing and is therefore the safer default.
#
# The enforced list ships instead as scripts/managed-settings.json, copied to
# /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json — root-owned, outside every mount, and read
# ahead of user settings.
.claude/settings.json
